# `definition` — what is still open

The feature is BUILT and in use (22 std files cite `[by definition …]`); the syntax,
semantics, `--axioms` disclosure and `--library` rule are documented in GUIDE.md under
`KEYWORD: definition`. What follows is only the part that was never decided.

## FUTURE: a SOUNDNESS CHECK on definitions (user, 2026-09-24 — flagged, NOT to build now)

This is the justification the two forms deserve, and it is better than "one is
shorter". Make the choice of form carry SEMANTIC weight:

  - **the definitional form gets a soundness check** — the checker verifies the
    clauses actually define something;
  - **the manual axiom form gets none** — you are asserting on your own
    authority, and you can therefore state an UNSOUND pair and have it accepted.

A reader then learns something real from the form: `func add(…) => Nat:` means
the checker vouched for those clauses; two hand-written axioms mean it did not.
Today the forms differ only in convenience, which is a weak reason for a language
to have both.

**What the check would have to mean, at minimum:**

1. **Pairwise consistency.** Two clauses whose guards can both hold must AGREE
   where they overlap. This is exactly the hazard we identified when settling
   "all conditions explicit on all clauses" and then deferred:

       gcd(a, b) = a                  when b = ZERO;
       gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, mod(a, b))            -- unguarded: overlaps at b = ZERO
                                                -- and contradicts the first

   The current answer is "the author's responsibility, unchecked, exactly as for
   hand-written axiom pairs". A soundness check is what would change that.

2. **Termination of recursive clauses.** The STRUCTURAL-DECREASE criterion worked
   out earlier: `add(succ(a), b) = succ(add(a, b))` decreases in its first
   argument, so the equation is well-founded; `f(n) = f(n)` or
   `f(n) = succ(f(n))` do not, and the second is outright inconsistent. This is
   the property that makes an equation a DEFINITION rather than a circular
   assertion — and note it is a genuine criterion, not a heuristic: it is exactly
   what makes the recursion well-founded.

Neither is cheap. Consistency needs the guards decidable enough to test for
overlap; termination is a real analysis. Both are, however, precisely the
properties that distinguish a definition from an axiom — which is why the
distinction is worth building rather than left as a style preference.

Open sub-question if this is taken up: is a failed check an ERROR (the definition
is rejected, and you must use the axiom form to say it anyway), or does it
DOWNGRADE (accepted, but reported as un-vouched)? The no-warnings philosophy
argues for the error; the "don't reject much" ruling argues for accepting what
converts to an axiom easily. They point opposite ways here.

## Open questions

- **Underdetermined binders.** Binder sorts are inferred lazily from the body,
  which usually determines them (in `isEvenPerm(g)`, the body's
  `productUpTo(s, m) = g` pins `g : Grp` via productUpTo's result sort). But a
  body can leave one free — the clearest case is equality between two binders and
  nothing else:

      pred sameThing(x, y):
        x = y

  Equality is sort-polymorphic, so the body constrains only that `x` and `y`
  MATCH; a first-order kernel needs a concrete sort. Options: (a) hard error at
  first demand, naming the binder; (b) an OPTIONAL annotation as the escape hatch
  (`pred sameThing(x: Element, y: Element):`), full inference otherwise;
  (c) require annotations always, which throws away the inference that motivated
  the feature. Leaning (b) — it matches how the language already works
  (declarations carry sorts, expressions are checked), keeping the common case
  clean and the awkward case expressible rather than blocked.

  Subtlety: because inference is LAZY and the signature is FIXED at first demand,
  an underdetermined definition is diagnosed only when something first cites it,
  and the error points at the DEFINITION rather than the citation. That is the
  right place for it, but it means an UNCITED bad definition is never diagnosed —
  consistent with how uncited files already behave, and now also covered by the
  `--library` rule, which forces every definition in a library to be used.
